  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Tranquil, dry weather will prevail through \nSaturday with no significant weather hazards anticipated.\n\nHigh pressure will build in today in the wake of a weak cold\nfront, bringing more dry weather. Highs today will generally be \nin the low to mid 70s so not too far off from average for this \ntime of year. Some cirrus has already started to move into\nsouthern areas and this will likely be creeping northward as the\nday progresses, but sunshine should get through it for the most\npart.\n\nThis evening, the center of high pressure will be centered \nright over the area but will then start to gradually retreat \neast and northeastward towards New England heading into \nSaturday. Expect increasing mid and high level cloudiness \nthrough the day Saturday as moisture starts to stream northward \nin the upper levels. However it remains dry through the day. \nMost areas will see highs similar to Friday however the winds \nwill be coming around to easterly on the south side of the high \nto our north so this will keep it a bit cooler near the coast. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The remnants of what is now Hurricane Isaias \nwill approach the area late this weekend bringing rain and a few\nthunderstorms back to the region Sunday and Sunday night.\n\nThe primary forecast concern for the latter part of this \nweekend into early next week will be the remnants of what is \ncurrently Hurricane Isaias in the Gulf. It may intensify \nfurther before making landfall somewhere along the northern Gulf\ncoast tonight as it begins interacting with an upper trough. \nFrom there, it is expected to undergo extratropical transition \nand continue northward towards portions of the Ohio Valley \nthrough Saturday night, and then northeastward in the direction \nof our area on Sunday. \n\nSome modest timing discrepancies remain amongst model guidance \nthis morning, but there is strong agreement that the remnants of\nIsaias will bring some impacts to the region beginning Sunday \nand perhaps lingering into early Monday. At this point in time, \nit appears that widespread rain will be the primary impact. It \nshould be noted that PWATs are modeled to be in the 1.5-2+ inch \nrange, so some periods of heavy rainfall will be possible. Our \ncurrent forecast is for storm totals in the 1.0 to 2.0 inch \nrange. Some areas could see over 2.0 inches, with the best\nchance of higher totals presently located in the Delmarva and \nsouthern New Jersey. WPC has placed most of the region in a \nMarginal Risk (1 of 4) Excessive Rainfall Outlook for Sunday \nmeaning at least isolated instances of flooding will be \npossible. We will continue to monitor trends over the coming \ndays.\n\nAside from the rain potential, with the system lifting over our\nregion, shear and CAPE may reach values just high enough to \nsupport some isolated severe storms Sunday/Sunday night, so our \nsouthern zones have been placed in a Marginal Risk (1 of 5) \nSevere Thunderstorm Outlook for Sunday. \n\nFinally, the easterly and southeasterly surface flow preceeding\nand during the passage of the system may result in minor tidal\nflooding Sunday into Monday.",
